 Designing a biomimetic leg requires a quantitative benchmark of human muscle--joint behavior. This dissertation uses OpenSim \citep{delp_opensim_2007, seth_opensim_2011, seth_opensim_2018} and the Gait2392 model, which descends from the interactive lower-extremity model of \citet{delp_interactive_1990} and includes musculoskeletal geometry from \citet{hoy_musculoskeletal_1990}, planar knee kinematics from \citet{yamaguchi_planar_1989}, and a lower-limb model with 23 degrees of freedom and 54 musculotendon actuators \citep{arnold_model_2010}. Muscle moment arms, force-generating properties, and functional roles in such models have been characterized extensively \citep{zajac_determining_1989, brand_model_1982, menegaldo_moment_2004, millard_flexing_2013, sherman_what_2013, suderman_moving_2012}, and standard reporting conventions for kinematics and joint mechanics are established by the International Society of Biomechanics \citep{wu_for_1995, wu_isb_2002, wu_isb_2005, derrick_isb_2020}.
 
-The moment arm is the quantity that turns muscle force into joint torque, and two methods of obtaining it anchor the literature. The geometric method computes the arm directly from attachment-point data: the arm about a joint axis is the perpendicular distance from that axis to the muscle's line of action, evaluated at the configuration of interest. \citet{brand_model_1982} assembled the canonical lower-extremity attachment anatomy from cadaver measurements so that such computations could be made at all, and reported that anatomical variation and marking errors rarely shift calculated moment arms by more than twenty percent, usually by less than ten, while attachment data taken from a single unscaled specimen can bias the resulting force predictions substantially. The tendon-excursion method instead defines the arm as the sensitivity of musculotendon length to joint angle, $r = \partial l/\partial\theta$, obtained by perturbing a joint and differencing path lengths. \citet{menegaldo_moment_2004} fitted polynomial regressions of musculotendon lengths and three-dimensional moment arms across the joint space of a lower-limb model so that either quantity could be evaluated cheaply inside large simulations. Both methods deliver configuration-dependent quantities rather than constants, and the dependence compounds for multi-articular muscles, whose arm about one joint varies with the configuration of every joint they cross \citep{zajac_determining_1989, young_analyzing_2019}; the full-muscle rat hindlimb model of \citet{young_analyzing_2019} makes that adjacent-joint dependence a first-class result of its attachment-point technique. OpenSim evaluates moment arms as muscle-length sensitivities of this kind, so the moment arms used throughout this dissertation are properties of the Gait2392 geometry rather than measured inputs \citep{delp_opensim_2007, seth_opensim_2018}.
-
-The human knee is the joint where these definitions are stressed hardest. Its instantaneous center of rotation migrates posteriorly with flexion, a behavior captured by four-bar linkage descriptions of the joint \citep{romero_four-bar_2020, steele_biomimetic_2018}, and \citet{romero_four-bar_2020} treat locating that moving center as a kinematic problem in its own right, solved with natural coordinates so that the answer stays cheap to evaluate when an optimization must recompute it many times. That difficulty is visible at the repository level: the SimTK repository that distributes OpenSim also hosts multiple projects dedicated specifically to the human knee, and they differ in how they formalize joint torques, centers of rotation, and reaction forces for the same anatomy. A hinge knee, a four-bar knee, and a contact-defined tibiofemoral joint give different moment arms for identical muscle paths, so the modeling choice must be determined carefully. The ISB joint coordinate system recommendations were written against exactly this definitional freedom, to keep reports of joint motion comparable across laboratories \citep{wu_isb_2002}. Throughout this dissertation, Gait2392 serves as the reference against which the robot's muscle routing, moment arms, and torque capabilities are designed and judged.
