+
+The human knee is the joint where these definitions are stressed hardest. Its instantaneous center of rotation migrates posteriorly with flexion, a behavior captured by four-bar linkage descriptions of the joint \citep{romero_four-bar_2020, steele_biomimetic_2018}, and \citet{romero_four-bar_2020} treat locating that moving center as a kinematic problem in its own right, solved with natural coordinates so that the answer stays cheap to evaluate when an optimization must recompute it many times. That difficulty is visible at the repository level: the SimTK repository that distributes OpenSim also hosts multiple projects dedicated specifically to the human knee, and they differ in how they formalize joint torques, centers of rotation, and reaction forces for the same anatomy. A hinge knee, a four-bar knee, and a contact-defined tibiofemoral joint give different moment arms for identical muscle paths, so the modeling choice must be matched to the question asked of it. The ISB joint coordinate system recommendations were written against exactly this definitional freedom, to keep reports of joint motion comparable across laboratories \citep{wu_isb_2002}. Throughout this dissertation, Gait2392 serves as the reference against which the robot's muscle routing, moment arms, and torque capabilities are designed and judged.
 
